adjudication.rationale:
The new helper `parseRefreshTokenResponse` explicitly overwrites a missing `refresh_token` with the literal string `"refresh_token"`:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

In `Office365CalendarService`, the parsed token data is merged back into `o365AuthCredentials` and then persisted to the DB via `prisma.credential.update(...)`. If Microsoft’s refresh response omits `refresh_token` (a common case), this code will replace the previously valid stored refresh token with the placeholder string, causing subsequent refresh attempts (which use `o365AuthCredentials.refresh_token`) to send an invalid refresh token and fail.

So the finding points to a concrete correctness bug introduced by this diff.

In the new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the decrypted payload is handled as:

```ts
const keys = JSON.parse(
  symmetricDecrypt(reqBody.keys, process.env.CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY || "")
);
...
await prisma.credential.update({ data: { key: keys } })
// or
await prisma.credential.create({ data: { key: keys, ... } })
```

There is no validation of `keys` against any expected schema for the given `appSlug`/`appMetadata.type` before persisting it. The only validation performed is on the outer request body (`userId`, `appSlug`, `keys` as a string). Therefore, an arbitrary JSON structure can be stored into `credential.key` via this webhook endpoint, which can lead to invalid/garbage credential records and downstream breakage in code that expects a specific credential shape.

In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook secret check is implemented as a direct inequality comparison:

```ts
if (req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This is not a constant-time comparison; JavaScript string equality/inequality can short-circuit based on the first differing character, so the time to evaluate can vary with how much of the secret matches. That is the exact pattern the finding calls out, and a constant-time comparison (e.g., `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on normalized buffers) would be required to avoid timing side-channels.
